import AxQM.Concrete.PhaseDampingHarmonicOscillator

/-!
# Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 8.31 (exponential sensitivity to phase damping)

*(N&C p. 386.)*

Show harmonic-oscillator element ρ_nm=⟨n|ρ|m⟩ decays as exp(-λ(n-m)²) under phase damping.

## The answer

N&C's eq. (8.126) is the phase-damping Hamiltonian `H = χ a†a (b + b†)`, whose system factor is the
number operator `N = a†a` (`N|n⟩ = n|n⟩` on the oscillator's number states). Coupling to the
environment turns the `n`-dependent phase into a random phase kick `D(θ) = exp(-i θ N)` with `θ`
Gaussian of mean `0` and variance `2λ` — this is N&C's model of eq. (8.124), now generated by the
number operator rather than the single-qubit `R_z`. The phase-damped density operator is the average
`E(ρ) = (4πλ)^{-1/2} ∫_ℝ D(θ) ρ D(θ)† e^{-θ²/(4λ)} dθ`. Because `D(θ)` is diagonal in the number
basis, conjugation multiplies the `(n,m)` entry by `e^{-i(n-m)θ}`, and the Gaussian average of that
phase — the Fourier transform of a Gaussian — is exactly `e^{-λ(n-m)²}`:

`⟨n| E(ρ) |m⟩ = e^{-λ(n-m)²} · ρ_nm`.

The coherence between number states decays super-exponentially in their energy difference `|n-m|`
(N&C Box 8.4: "coherence between states of high energy difference decays faster"), while the
populations `ρ_nn` are untouched. The constant `λ` is fixed by the Gaussian variance.

## The formalisation

The harmonic oscillator's state space is the infinite-dimensional Fock space `ℓ²(ℕ)` (number states
`|n⟩`, `n : ℕ`), which is not an `AxQM.QSystem` (which bundles a *finite*-dimensional Hilbert space,
`QSystem.findim`); compare Exercise 8.21 (`AxQM/Concrete/HarmonicOscillatorAmplitudeDamping.lean`).
The formal content is pure mathematics about number-basis density-matrix elements (`Matrix ℕ ℕ ℂ`,
`Complex.exp`, `Real.exp`, a Lebesgue integral over `ℝ`). Compare Exercises 8.21 and 8.26.

The formalisation is in `AxQM/Concrete/PhaseDampingHarmonicOscillator.lean`:

* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampingDecayFactor` — the decay factor `e^{-λ k²}` multiplying the
  coherence between number states differing by `k = n - m` quanta, with
  `phaseDampingDecayFactor_zero` (`= 1` on the diagonal), `phaseDampingDecayFactor_pos`,
  `phaseDampingDecayFactor_le_one`, and `phaseDampingDecayFactor_antitone_sq` — **Box 8.4:** a
  larger energy difference `|n-m|` gives a smaller (faster-decaying) coherence.
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseKickAverage` — the **quantitative heart**: the Gaussian
  characteristic function `(4πλ)^{-1/2} ∫_ℝ e^{-i k θ} e^{-θ²/(4λ)} dθ = e^{-λ k²}` (the Fourier
  transform of a Gaussian, `fourierIntegral_gaussian`). Evaluating this integral *is* the exercise.
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseKickElem` — the phase-kicked density-matrix element
  `⟨n| D(θ) ρ D(θ)† |m⟩` for the number-operator phase kick `D(θ) = exp(-i θ N)`, with
  `phaseKickElem_eq` (`= e^{-i(n-m)θ} ρ_nm`: the kick affects only the relative phase).
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampedElem` — the phase-damped element `⟨n| E(ρ) |m⟩`, the Gaussian
  average of the phase-kicked element.
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampedElem_eq` — **Exercise 8.31 (headline):**
  `⟨n| E(ρ) |m⟩ = e^{-λ(n-m)²} · ρ_nm`, the exponential decay of the density-matrix element.
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampedElem_diagonal` — the populations `ρ_nn` are preserved
  (`⟨n| E(ρ) |n⟩ = ρ_nn`): phase damping loses coherence without loss of energy.
* `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampedElem_qubit_offDiag` /
  `AxQM.Concrete.phaseDampedElem_qubit_offDiag'` — the **qubit specialization recovering
  N&C eq. (8.125):** the `(0,1)` and `(1,0)` coherences are damped by exactly `e^{-λ}` (the
  `|n-m| = 1` case), so together with `phaseDampedElem_diagonal` the general oscillator formula
  reduces to the
  book's explicit qubit density matrix `[[|a|², a b* e^{-λ}], [a* b e^{-λ}, |b|²]]`. Also relevant
  are the decay-factor facts `phaseDampingDecayFactor_neg` (even in the gap `k = n-m`, so
  Hermiticity is preserved) and `phaseDampingDecayFactor_one` (`= e^{-λ}` at `|k|=1`).

See the module docstring of `AxQM/Concrete/PhaseDampingHarmonicOscillator.lean` for the full
argument, including why the microscopic derivation of eq. (8.126) via displacement operators is not
part of the library.
-/
